# What the image build is allowed to see.
#
# Docker sends this directory to the builder before the first instruction runs, so anything
# not excluded here is readable by every build stage — including stages whose output is
# discarded. A file that never enters the context cannot be copied into a layer by mistake.
#
# Ordered: secrets and state first, because those are the exclusions that matter.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and runtime state — must never enter the build context
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets.json
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*

# The SQLite store and its WAL/SHM sidecars. These live on a bind mount in
# production; a copy baked into an image would be a stale, shipped database.
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Deployment state, logs, and project workspaces, wherever a local run put them.
/data/
/var/
/logs/
*.log

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reinstalled or regenerated inside the build
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules
.next
out
build
dist
coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not needed to build the application
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github
.husky
docs
README.md
*.md
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Tooling that only runs on a developer machine or in CI.
#
# The test and lint configs are deliberately *not* excluded: `next build` type-checks
# every file `tsconfig.json` includes, and removing some of them from the context would
# make the container build check a different set of files than `npm run build` does.
.editorconfig
.prettierrc.json
.prettierignore
.lintstagedrc.json
.claude
.vscode
.idea
*.iml

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
